If Plugin.trigger throws an exception (e.g., due to a buggy plugin), the shell resolution will fail and propagate the error, potentially breaking command execution. Consider wrapping the Plugin.trigger call in a try-catch to gracefully fall back to the next resolution step (environment variable or system default) when a plugin hook fails, ensuring more robust shell resolution even with misbehaving plugins.
```suggestion
    try {
      await Plugin.trigger("shell.resolve", { platform: process.platform }, result)
    } catch (_e) {
      // Ignore plugin errors and allow fallback to environment or default shell
    }
```

Potential inconsistency: `$HOME` without a trailing slash is handled (line 17), but `~` without a trailing slash is not. If someone configures `{ "~": "allow" }`, it wont be expanded. Consider adding a check for just `~` to match the `$HOME` behavior:

```suggestion
  function expand(pattern: string): string {
    if (pattern.startsWith("~/")) return os.homedir() + pattern.slice(1)
    if (pattern === "~") return os.homedir()
    if (pattern.startsWith("$HOME/")) return os.homedir() + pattern.slice(5)
    if (pattern.startsWith("$HOME")) return os.homedir() + pattern.slice(5)
    return pattern
  }
```

