The command construction in the `exec` function is vulnerable to command injection. The `options.cwd` is used with `JSON.stringify` but then interpolated directly into the shell command. Similarly, environment variable values are stringified but keys are not validated. If `options.cwd` contains malicious content or if environment variable keys contain special characters, this could lead to command injection. Consider using a more robust shell escaping mechanism.
```suggestion
    // Escape a single shell argument for POSIX shells by wrapping in single quotes
    // and escaping any embedded single quotes.
    const shellEscapeArg = (arg: string): string =>
      `'${arg.replace(/'/g, `'\\''`)}'`

    const isSafeEnvKey = (key: string): boolean =>
      /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/.test(key)

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      let fullCommand = command
      if (options?.cwd) {
        const escapedCwd = shellEscapeArg(options.cwd)
        fullCommand = `cd ${escapedCwd} && ${command}`
      }

      if (options?.env) {
        const envStr = Object.entries(options.env)
          .map(([k, v]) => {
            if (!isSafeEnvKey(k)) {
              throw new Error(`Invalid environment variable name: ${k}`)
            }
            const escapedValue = shellEscapeArg(String(v))
            return `export ${k}=${escapedValue}`
          })
```

